  "plan": {
    "goal": "Blood-brain barrier perfusion channel reproducing physiologic brain microvascular endothelial shear (1.0 Pa) with mean fluid residence time ≥ 6 s for a drug perfusion study.",
    "rationale": "hCMEC/D3 (human brain microvascular endothelial) cells experience physiological brain capillary shear; 1.0 Pa is a defensible target for a BBB-on-chip. The requested 400×100 µm × 100 mm channel has a fixed volume of 4 µL, so residence time τ = 4 µL/Q is locked to shear (both ∝ Q): at the 40 µL/min needed for 1.0 Pa, τ = 6.0 s exactly — at the threshold, not above it. To satisfy BOTH constraints with margin, the channel length is increased to 150 mm (volume 6 µL), giving τ = 9.0 s at the same 40 µL/min / 1.0 Pa. Reynolds = 2.67 (laminar) and pressure drop = 3 kPa (within syringe-pump range). Note: hCMEC/D3 forms only a moderately restrictive barrier (TEER 100–240 Ω·cm², ~10× below physiological BBB); if a tight barrier is required, consider co-culture with astrocytes/pericytes or a primary cell source.",
    "chip": {
      "width_um": 400.0,
      "height_um": 100.0,
      "length_mm": 150.0,
      "channel_count": 1,
      "material": "PDMS"
    },
